    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This thesis explores the use of deep learning techniques for detecting leaks in water pipelines through binary and multi-class classification models. The methods discussed address challenges related to balancing bias and variance in model development and deployment. Techniques such as dropouts, L2 regularization, and proper sizing of neural network architectures are considered. The study compares the performance of different models including Random Forest (RF), Feedforward Neural Networks (FNNs), Gated Recurrent Units (GRUs), and Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks.\r\n\r\nExtensive experiments demonstrate that model performance improves after feature engineering. Generally, deep learning models, particularly FNNs, GRUs, and LSTM, exhibit better generalization capabilities than the RF model. For binary classification tasks, the LSTM model achieves the highest accuracy and F1 score, indicating its ability to effectively represent temporal dependence in the dataset. Additionally, for multi-class classification, the LSTM model outperforms others, with each class being considered 20\\% of the time. However, model selection and feature engineering are crucial factors in designing a reliable water pipeline leakage detection system. Detailed trade-offs and benefits between different deep learning-based architectures and techniques must be explored for practical applications to enhance the reliability and efficiency of water distribution systems.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
